WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.110


00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.159
Understand how the economy works in 30 minutes. The

00:00:03.159 --> 00:00:06.680
economy operates like a simple machine,

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:09.390
but many people don't understand this

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:11.490
or hold different views on how the economy works, leading

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:15.150
to many unnecessary economic losses.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:18.249
I feel a deep responsibility to

00:00:18.249 --> 00:00:22.049
share my simple yet practical economic analysis model.

00:00:23.049 --> 00:00:25.320
Although this model does not conform to conventional traditional economics, it

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:30.150
has helped me predict and avoid the global financial crisis and has been

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.460
useful to me for over 30 years.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.260
Let's begin.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:39.520
Although the economy may seem complex, it actually operates in a simple and mechanical way. The

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.930
economy consists of a few simple components and transactions that are

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:47.260
constantly repeated, cycling countless times.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:51.460
These transactions are primarily driven by human nature,

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:54.800
thus forming three main economic drivers:

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.120
1. Productivity growth;

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:01.650
2. Short-term debt cycle;

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:04.400
3. Long-term debt cycle.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.770
Below, we will discuss these three drivers and explain how to combine them to

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:11.530
create a model for tracking economic trends

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:13.590
and understanding what is currently happening.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:16.830
Let's start with the simplest part of the economy:

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.860
transactions. The

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:23.840
economy is nothing more than the sum of countless transactions,

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.570
and transactions are a very simple thing.

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:28.360
You are constantly making transactions.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.760
Every time you buy something, you create a transaction.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.240
Each transaction involves a buyer who

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.150
uses currency or credit to exchange for the

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:42.550
seller's goods, services, or financial assets.

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:45.150
Credit is used in the same way as money.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:49.280
Adding the money and credit spent together gives you the

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.160
total expenditure. Total expenditure

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:55.560
is the driving force of the economy.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.590
Dividing the expenditure

00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:59.990
by the sales volume

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:01.290
gives you the price.

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:04.380
It's that simple. This is transaction.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.110
Transaction is the most basic component of the economic machine.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:12.019
All economic cycles and dynamics are driven by transactions.

00:02:12.019 --> 00:02:14.469
Therefore, if we can understand transactions,

00:02:14.469 --> 00:02:16.569
we can understand the entire economy.

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:19.510
A market contains all buyers

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:20.510
and all sellers

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:22.910
trading the same commodity.

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:25.540
For example, there are wheat markets,

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:26.840
automobile markets,

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:27.640
stock markets,

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:29.550
and millions of other markets.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.470
An economy is

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:34.670
made up of all the transactions that take place within these markets.

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:37.550
If you add up

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:39.450
the information on total spending and total sales

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:40.430
from all the markets,

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:42.030
you have all the information

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:43.630
to understand how the economy works

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:45.960
. It's that simple.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:49.430
Individuals, businesses, banks, and governments all

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:53.730
transact in the manner described above,

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:58.530
exchanging goods, services, and financial assets using money and credit.

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:01.500
Governments are the largest buyers and sellers.

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:04.040
Governments consist of two parts: the

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:07.690
central government, which collects taxes and spends money,

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:09.669
and the central bank. The

00:03:09.669 --> 00:03:12.269
central bank differs from other buyers and sellers

00:03:12.269 --> 00:03:15.989
because it controls the amount of money and credit in the economy. The central

00:03:16.689 --> 00:03:19.650
bank exercises this control by influencing interest rates

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:21.020
and issuing more money.

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:23.620
For this reason, as we will see below, the

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:27.200
central bank plays a crucial role in the flow of credit.

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:28.700


00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.629
Please pay close attention to credit.

00:03:31.629 --> 00:03:34.659
Credit is the most important component of the economy,

00:03:34.659 --> 00:03:36.539
but perhaps the least understood.

00:03:37.039 --> 00:03:40.319
It is most important because it is the largest

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:42.100
and most volatile part of the economy.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:45.100
Just as buyers and sellers enter the market to transact, so do

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.969
lenders and borrowers.

00:03:49.969 --> 00:03:53.569
Typically, lenders want their money to generate more money,

00:03:53.569 --> 00:03:57.260
while borrowers want to buy something they can't afford right now,

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:59.059
such as a house or a car,

00:03:59.059 --> 00:04:02.319
or make an investment, such as starting a business.

00:04:03.019 --> 00:04:04.599
Credit can help lenders

00:04:04.599 --> 00:04:07.799
and borrowers get what they want. A

00:04:08.099 --> 00:04:11.560
borrower promises to repay a loan,

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:12.719
called the principal,

00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:15.419
and pays additional amounts, called interest.

00:04:15.419 --> 00:04:17.560
When interest rates are high,

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:20.560
borrowing decreases because loans become expensive.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.540
When interest rates are low,

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:25.040
borrowing increases because loans become cheaper.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:27.930
If a borrower guarantees to repay a debt,

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:29.959
and the lender believes

00:04:29.959 --> 00:04:31.059
this promise, credit is created.

00:04:31.659 --> 00:04:35.170
Any two people can create credit out of thin air through an agreement!

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:39.210
Credit may seem simple, but it is actually quite cunning

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:40.499
because it has different names.

00:04:40.499 --> 00:04:43.599
Once credit is created,

00:04:43.599 --> 00:04:45.029
it immediately becomes debt.

00:04:46.229 --> 00:04:48.670
Debt is both an asset for the lender

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:50.770
and a liability for the borrower.

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:52.540
In the future,

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:56.240
once the borrower repays the loan and pays the interest,

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.549
these assets and liabilities will disappear,

00:04:58.549 --> 00:05:00.949
and the transaction will be completed.

00:05:01.749 --> 00:05:04.629
So why is credit so important?

00:05:05.229 --> 00:05:07.619
Because once a borrower obtains credit,

00:05:07.619 --> 00:05:09.719
he can increase his consumption expenditure.

00:05:09.719 --> 00:05:13.349
Don't forget that consumer spending is a driver of the economy.

00:05:13.349 --> 00:05:15.699
This is because one person's consumption

00:05:15.699 --> 00:05:17.099
becomes another person's income.

00:05:17.499 --> 00:05:22.189
Think about it, for every dollar you spend, someone else earns a dollar.

00:05:22.189 --> 00:05:25.279
And every dollar you earn is inevitably someone else's consumption.

00:05:25.279 --> 00:05:29.149
Therefore, when you spend more, others earn more.

00:05:29.149 --> 00:05:32.649
When someone's income increases,

00:05:32.649 --> 00:05:34.649
lenders are more willing to lend to them

00:05:34.649 --> 00:05:37.629
because their creditworthiness improves.

00:05:37.629 --> 00:05:40.929
Borrowers with good credit have two qualities:

00:05:40.929 --> 00:05:43.519
repayment ability and collateral. A

00:05:44.319 --> 00:05:48.319
higher income-to-debt ratio means the borrower has the ability to repay.

00:05:49.079 --> 00:05:55.749
If unable to repay, the borrower can use valuable, marketable assets as collateral

00:05:55.749 --> 00:06:00.340
. This allows lenders to lend money with confidence.

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:03.960
Therefore, increased income leads to increased borrowing, which

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:05.680
in turn increases spending. Since one person's spending

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:09.920
is another person's income,

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:13.360
this leads to further increases in borrowing, creating a continuous cycle.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:17.350
This self-driven pattern leads to economic growth,

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:21.640
and it is precisely because of this that economic cycles exist.

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:25.620
In a transaction, you must give up something else to obtain something else.

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:28.830
How much you get depends on how much you can produce.

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:31.430
Our knowledge gradually increases over time. The

00:06:31.430 --> 00:06:34.680
accumulation of knowledge improves our standard of living;

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:36.930
we call this increased productivity.

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:40.430
Those who are innovative and diligent will improve productivity and standard of living faster

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:43.540
than those who are complacent and lazy,

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:46.950


00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:49.710
but this may not be apparent in the short term. Over a

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:54.340
long period, productivity is the most crucial factor, but credit is most important in the short term.

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:58.370
This is because increased productivity does not fluctuate dramatically and is

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:01.480
therefore not a major driver of economic fluctuations. However,

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:06.750
debt is the driving force behind this phenomenon because we can borrow to make consumption exceed output,

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:11.050
but when repaying the debt, we have to make consumption fall below output. The

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:14.770
fluctuations in debt levels have two major cycles:

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:20.830
one lasting approximately 5 to 8 years, and the other approximately 75 to 100 years.

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:25.160
Most people can feel the fluctuations, but because they are so close to them,

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:29.490
experiencing them daily and weekly, they usually don't recognize them as cycles.

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:32.950
In this chapter, we will examine these three main driving forces

00:07:32.950 --> 00:07:37.000
and observe how they interact and how they perform in the everyday economy.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:42.340
As mentioned above, economic ups and downs do not depend on how innovative or hardworking people are,

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:46.240
but primarily on the total amount of credit.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:50.070
Let's imagine an economy without credit.

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:53.810
In such an economy, the only way to increase spending is to

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:55.480
increase income.

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:59.080
Therefore, productivity and workload need to be increased.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:03.020
Increasing productivity is the only way for economic growth,

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:05.250
since my spending is someone else's income.

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:10.430
When I or someone else increases productivity, the economy grows.

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:13.530
If we observe various transactions and summarize them,

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:16.910
we will find an asymptotic line similar to the trajectory of productivity growth.

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:20.780
However, because we borrow, cycles arise.

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:23.900
The reason is not any regulation,

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:27.660
but human nature and how credit works. Borrowing is simply

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:32.270
advance consumption.

00:08:32.270 --> 00:08:37.479
To buy things you can't afford now, your spending inevitably exceeds your income.

00:08:37.479 --> 00:08:41.460
For this reason, you need to borrow money—essentially borrowing from your future self.

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:44.610
You set a future timeframe for yourself, at which point your spending must be

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:48.690
less than your income to repay the debt.

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:51.950
This immediately creates a cycle.

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:55.400
Generally, once you borrow money, you create a cycle.

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:59.950
This is true for individuals, and it's true for the entire economy.

00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:01.850
This is why it's crucial to understand credit.

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:04.590
Because credit triggers

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:08.790
a series of mechanical and predictable events that will occur in the future.

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:11.570
This is where credit differs from money. Transactions

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:14.580
require money.

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:18.120
When you buy a beer at a bar with cash,

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:20.800
the transaction is complete immediately.

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:22.630
But if you buy a beer on credit,

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:24.730
like on credit,

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:27.530
you're essentially promising to pay for that beer in the future.

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:32.070
You and the bar create an asset and a liability together.

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:35.250
You create credit out of thin air.

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:38.900
Only after you repay the debt in the future

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:41.250
will the asset and liability disappear,

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:42.550
the debt be paid off, and

00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:44.090
the transaction be closed.

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:50.250
In real life, most so-called money is actually credit.

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:55.510
The total amount of credit in the United States is approximately $50 trillion,

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:59.680
while the total money supply is only about $3 trillion. Don't

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:02.040
forget, in an economy without credit, the

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:05.200
only way to increase spending is to increase production.

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:07.280
But in an economy with credit…

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:10.780
Spending can also be increased through borrowing.

00:10:10.780 --> 00:10:14.120
Therefore, an economy with credit can increase spending,

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:18.090
causing income growth to outpace productivity growth in the short term,

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:19.890
but not in the long term.

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:21.360
However, please don't misunderstand me;

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:25.660
credit isn't necessarily bad, it just leads to cyclical fluctuations. Credit that results in

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:30.900
excessive consumption beyond repayment capacity is bad credit.

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:34.490
But credit can be benign credit, if it allocates resources more efficiently

00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:37.690
and generates more income to help you repay debt.

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:40.980
For example, if you borrow money to buy a large television,

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:43.879
the television itself won't generate any income to help you repay the debt.

00:10:44.079 --> 00:10:47.880
However, if you borrow money to buy a tractor,

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:51.560
use it to harvest more crops, earn more money, and

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:53.090
you can repay the debt

00:10:53.090 --> 00:10:56.490
and improve your standard of living.

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:57.600
In an economy with credit,

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:59.600
we can track various transactions

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:01.300
to observe how credit drives economic growth.

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:03.680
Let me give you an example:

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:08.190
Suppose you earn $100,000 a year with no debt.

00:11:08.390 --> 00:11:11.540
You have good credit and can borrow $10,000,

00:11:11.540 --> 00:11:13.040
for example, with a credit card.

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:15.250
Therefore, you can spend $110,000 a year,

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:18.250
even though your income is only $100,000.

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:21.460
Meanwhile, your consumption becomes someone else's income, and that

00:11:21.460 --> 00:11:24.460
person earns $110,000.

00:11:25.060 --> 00:11:27.670
This person's income is $110,000.

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:31.429
If this person has no debt, they can borrow $11,000 and then spend $121,000,

00:11:31.429 --> 00:11:34.070


00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:38.170
even if their annual income is only $110,000. This is

00:11:38.170 --> 00:11:40.640
because their spending becomes someone else's income. By

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:43.430
tracking these transactions, we can begin to see how

00:11:43.430 --> 00:11:45.430
this process is

00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:47.430
constantly reinforcing itself.

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:51.600
But don't forget, borrowing forms cycles; cycles rise and eventually fall. Now let's

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:55.809


00:11:56.009 --> 00:11:59.710
talk about the short-term debt cycle.

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:04.380
With increased economic activity, expansion occurs.

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:06.840
This is the first stage of the short-term debt cycle.

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:10.720
Spending continues to increase, and prices begin to rise.

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:15.230
The reason is that what drives increased spending is credit,

00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:17.450
and credit can appear out of thin air.

00:12:17.450 --> 00:12:22.680
If the growth rate of spending and income exceeds the production rate of the goods sold,

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:23.880
prices will rise.

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:28.000
We call rising prices inflation.

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:32.500
Central banks don't want excessive inflation

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:35.010
because it leads to many problems.

00:12:35.010 --> 00:12:38.250
When central banks see rising prices, they raise interest rates.

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:42.959
As interest rates rise, fewer people are able to borrow money, and the

00:12:42.959 --> 00:12:45.600
cost of existing debt also increases.

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:50.100
Imagine your monthly credit card payment

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:54.459
increases. As people borrow less, payment amounts increase, leaving less money for

00:12:54.459 --> 00:12:58.720
spending, thus slowing down the pace of spending.

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:02.779
And since one person's spending becomes another

00:13:02.779 --> 00:13:06.589
person's income, this cycle repeats, leading to a decrease in income.

00:13:06.589 --> 00:13:10.800
As spending decreases, prices fall.

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:12.689
We call this deflation. Reduced

00:13:12.689 --> 00:13:17.460
economic activity leads to a recession.

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:20.060
If the recession becomes too severe

00:13:20.060 --> 00:13:22.440
and inflation ceases to be a problem,

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:27.060
the central bank will lower interest rates, causing economic activity to accelerate again.

00:13:27.060 --> 00:13:29.650
As interest rates fall,

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:30.650
debt servicing costs decrease.

00:13:30.650 --> 00:13:33.250
Borrowing and spending increase, leading

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:35.450
to another economic expansion.

00:13:35.450 --> 00:13:39.450
As you can see, the economy operates like a machine.

00:13:40.250 --> 00:13:44.160
In a short-term debt cycle, the only factor limiting spending is the

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:47.140
willingness of lenders and borrowers to lend and borrow.

00:13:47.140 --> 00:13:52.510
If credit is readily available, the economy expands.

00:13:52.510 --> 00:13:56.090
If credit is difficult to obtain, the economy declines.

00:13:56.090 --> 00:14:00.450
Note that this cycle is primarily controlled by the central bank.

00:14:00.450 --> 00:14:05.120
Short-term debt cycles typically last 5 to 8 years and

00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:08.410
repeat themselves over decades.

00:14:08.410 --> 00:14:10.410
However, note that after each cycle's trough

00:14:10.410 --> 00:14:12.400
and peak, both

00:14:12.400 --> 00:14:16.670
economic growth and debt exceed those of the previous cycle.

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:18.040
Why is this?

00:14:18.040 --> 00:14:20.740
This is driven by human nature.

00:14:20.740 --> 00:14:25.530
Humans have a tendency to borrow more money and spend more money, and dislike repaying debts.

00:14:25.530 --> 00:14:27.480
This is human nature.

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:29.380
Therefore,

00:14:29.380 --> 00:14:30.680
in the long run,

00:14:30.680 --> 00:14:33.470
debt will increase faster than income,

00:14:33.470 --> 00:14:36.670
thus creating a long-term debt cycle.

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:39.670
Despite the increase in people's debt,

00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:44.030
lenders will provide more lenient credit terms.

00:14:44.030 --> 00:14:45.030
Why is this?

00:14:45.030 --> 00:14:48.670
This is because everyone thought the situation was very promising!

00:14:48.670 --> 00:14:51.970
People only pay attention to recent

00:14:51.970 --> 00:14:55.650
events. What are recent events?

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:57.650
Income keeps increasing!

00:14:57.650 --> 00:14:59.250
Asset value continues to rise!

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:01.550
The stock market is booming!

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:02.480
This is a time of prosperity! It's a great deal to

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:06.690
use borrowed money to buy goods, services, and financial assets

00:15:06.690 --> 00:15:08.390
! A

00:15:08.390 --> 00:15:11.770
bubble is created when people over-borrow to consume. Therefore,

00:15:11.770 --> 00:15:14.640
although debt continues to increase,

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:18.110
income increases at a similar rate, thus offsetting the debt.

00:15:18.110 --> 00:15:21.970
We call the debt-to-income ratio the debt burden.

00:15:21.970 --> 00:15:25.210
As long as income continues to rise, the

00:15:25.210 --> 00:15:27.040
debt burden can be managed.

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:30.230
Meanwhile, asset values ​​rise rapidly.

00:15:30.230 --> 00:15:33.230
People borrow heavily

00:15:34.030 --> 00:15:35.060
to buy assets,

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:37.660
as investment drives up asset prices.

00:15:37.660 --> 00:15:40.260
People feel wealthy.

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:43.260
Therefore, despite accumulating large amounts of debt,

00:15:43.260 --> 00:15:49.490
rising income and asset values ​​help borrowers maintain good credit in the long run.

00:15:49.490 --> 00:15:52.530
However, this situation clearly cannot last forever,

00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:54.250
and indeed it hasn't. Over

00:15:54.250 --> 00:16:00.520
the decades, the debt burden has increased slowly, making debt servicing costs increasingly higher. At a

00:16:00.520 --> 00:16:05.400
certain point, the rate of increase in debt servicing costs exceeds that of income,

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:08.240
forcing people to cut back on spending.

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:12.200
Simultaneously, since one person's expenditure is another person's income,

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:14.500
income begins to decline, leading to a decrease in

00:16:14.500 --> 00:16:19.020
people's creditworthiness and a reduction in borrowing.

00:16:19.020 --> 00:16:22.020
Debt servicing costs continue to increase,

00:16:22.020 --> 00:16:24.440
causing further reductions in expenditure.

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:27.140
The cycle begins to reverse,

00:16:27.140 --> 00:16:30.960
reaching the peak of long-term debt. The

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:34.150
debt burden becomes excessive. This situation occurred in

00:16:34.150 --> 00:16:38.830
the United States, Europe, and many other parts of the world

00:16:38.830 --> 00:16:40.760
in 2008.

00:16:40.760 --> 00:16:45.430
Japan in 1989 and the United States as early as 1929

00:16:45.430 --> 00:16:48.750
also experienced this situation for the same reason.

00:16:48.750 --> 00:16:51.980
Now, the economy has entered a period of

00:16:51.980 --> 00:16:56.500
deleveraging. During deleveraging, people cut spending,

00:16:56.500 --> 00:16:59.540
income declines, credit disappears,

00:16:59.540 --> 00:17:02.700
asset prices fall, banks experience runs,

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:06.340
stock markets crash, and social tensions intensify.

00:17:06.340 --> 00:17:09.709
The entire process begins to decline and form a vicious cycle.

00:17:09.709 --> 00:17:13.850
As income declines and debt servicing costs increase,

00:17:13.850 --> 00:17:17.990
borrowers feel increasingly strapped for cash and no longer have good credit.

00:17:17.990 --> 00:17:22.360
Credit dries up, and borrowers can no longer borrow enough money to

00:17:22.360 --> 00:17:23.600
repay their debts.

00:17:23.600 --> 00:17:28.440
Borrowers desperately try to fill the hole, forced to sell assets.

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:31.550
As spending decreases, a selling frenzy floods the market with assets for sale. At

00:17:31.550 --> 00:17:36.370
this point, the stock market crashes, the

00:17:36.370 --> 00:17:39.740
real estate market collapses, and banks are in trouble.

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:44.940
As asset prices fall, the value of collateral borrowers can offer decreases.

00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:48.280
This further reduces borrowers' creditworthiness.

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:50.200
People feel poor.

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:55.010
Credit disappears rapidly, spending decreases,

00:17:55.010 --> 00:17:55.890
income decreases,

00:17:55.890 --> 00:17:57.890
wealth decreases,

00:17:57.890 --> 00:17:58.590
credit decreases, and

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:00.800
borrowing and lending decrease as well.

00:18:00.800 --> 00:18:03.000
It's a vicious cycle.

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:06.530
It may resemble a recession, but the difference is that

00:18:06.530 --> 00:18:10.160
lowering interest rates cannot reverse the situation.

00:18:10.160 --> 00:18:14.590
In a recession, lowering interest rates can stimulate borrowing.

00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:18.570
However, during deleveraging,

00:18:18.570 --> 00:18:20.530
interest rates are already very low, close to zero,

00:18:20.530 --> 00:18:25.310
thus losing their stimulating function, and lowering rates is ineffective.

00:18:25.310 --> 00:18:29.210
Domestic interest rates in the US fell to zero during the deleveraging of the 1930s, and

00:18:29.210 --> 00:18:30.840


00:18:30.840 --> 00:18:33.440
also in 2008. The

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:35.840
difference between a recession and deleveraging is that

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:40.270
during deleveraging, borrowers' debt burden

00:18:40.270 --> 00:18:41.930
becomes too heavy to be

00:18:41.930 --> 00:18:45.050
alleviated by lowering interest rates.

00:18:45.050 --> 00:18:50.240
Lenders realize that the debt is too large to repay in full.

00:18:50.240 --> 00:18:55.310
Borrowers lose their ability to repay, their collateral loses value, and

00:18:55.310 --> 00:18:59.370
they feel severely damaged by debt and do not want to borrow any more! When

00:18:59.370 --> 00:19:03.490
lenders stop lending and borrowers stop borrowing, the

00:19:03.490 --> 00:19:07.020
entire economy, like individuals,

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:09.000
loses its creditworthiness.

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:12.600
So how should we deal with deleveraging?

00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:17.540
The problem is that the debt burden is too heavy and must be reduced. There are

00:19:17.540 --> 00:19:20.790
four ways to do this:

00:19:20.790 --> 00:19:24.489
1. Individuals, businesses and governments cut spending.

00:19:24.489 --> 00:19:28.409
2. Reduce debt through default and restructuring.

00:19:28.409 --> 00:19:34.139
3. Wealth redistribution, transferring wealth from the rich to the poor.

00:19:34.139 --> 00:19:37.980
4. Finally, the central bank issues more currency.

00:19:37.980 --> 00:19:42.999
These four methods have been used in every deleveraging process in modern history.

00:19:45.699 --> 00:19:47.779
Usually, the first measure is to cut spending.

00:19:47.879 --> 00:19:52.129
As we just saw, individuals, businesses, banks, and governments tighten their belts and

00:19:52.129 --> 00:19:54.869
cut spending to be able to afford the debt. We

00:19:54.869 --> 00:19:58.559
often call this austerity.

00:19:58.559 --> 00:20:01.830
When borrowers stop taking in new debt

00:20:01.830 --> 00:20:06.769
and start paying off old debts, you might think the debt burden will decrease.

00:20:06.769 --> 00:20:10.720
But the situation is exactly the opposite! When spending decreases,

00:20:10.720 --> 00:20:14.610
and one person's spending is another person's income, this leads to a

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:19.029
decrease in income. Revenue is falling faster than debt is being repaid,

00:20:19.029 --> 00:20:23.210
so the debt burden is actually heavier. We have already seen

00:20:23.210 --> 00:20:26.330
this spending-cutting approach cause deflation, which is painful.

00:20:26.330 --> 00:20:29.389
Businesses have to cut costs,

00:20:29.389 --> 00:20:32.779
which means fewer jobs and higher unemployment.

00:20:32.779 --> 00:20:37.139
This leads to the next step: debt reduction!

00:20:37.139 --> 00:20:41.149
Many borrowers are unable to repay their loans,

00:20:41.149 --> 00:20:44.299
and these debts are the assets of the lenders.

00:20:44.299 --> 00:20:48.749
If borrowers default on bank loans, people worry that banks

00:20:48.749 --> 00:20:50.309
won't be able to return their deposits, leading to

00:20:50.309 --> 00:20:54.909
bank runs and defaults by

00:20:54.909 --> 00:20:55.590
individuals,

00:20:55.590 --> 00:21:00.080
businesses, and banks. This severe

00:21:00.080 --> 00:21:03.570
economic contraction is a depression.

00:21:03.570 --> 00:21:08.649
A key characteristic of a depression is that people discover a

00:21:08.649 --> 00:21:11.089
large portion of what they thought was their wealth doesn't actually exist. Let's take a

00:21:11.089 --> 00:21:13.490
bar as an example again.

00:21:13.690 --> 00:21:17.619
When you buy a beer on credit, you

00:21:17.619 --> 00:21:23.570
're promising to repay the bar later; your promise becomes an asset of the bar.

00:21:23.570 --> 00:21:28.129
However, if you don't keep your promise and don't repay the bar, you're defaulting on the debt. The

00:21:28.129 --> 00:21:29.309


00:21:29.309 --> 00:21:32.649
bar's "asset" is essentially worthless;

00:21:32.649 --> 00:21:35.779
it has effectively disappeared.

00:21:35.779 --> 00:21:39.710
Many borrowers don't want their assets to disappear and

00:21:39.710 --> 00:21:40.759
agree to debt restructuring.

00:21:40.759 --> 00:21:44.320
Debt restructuring means the borrower receives

00:21:44.320 --> 00:21:48.049
less repayment, the repayment period is extended,

00:21:48.049 --> 00:21:52.330
or the interest rate is lower than initially agreed. In any case,

00:21:52.330 --> 00:21:56.889
the contract is broken, resulting in reduced debt. The borrower hopes to recover

00:21:56.889 --> 00:21:59.419
some of the loan, which is better than losing everything.

00:21:59.419 --> 00:22:03.450
Although debt restructuring makes the debt disappear, it causes

00:22:03.450 --> 00:22:06.559
income and asset value to disappear at an even faster rate,

00:22:06.559 --> 00:22:09.679
so the debt burden continues to grow heavier.

00:22:09.679 --> 00:22:12.950
Debt reduction, like spending cuts, is

00:22:12.950 --> 00:22:16.009
painful and can lead to deflation.

00:22:16.009 --> 00:22:21.940
All of this impacts the central government because lower incomes and fewer jobs

00:22:21.940 --> 00:22:27.049
mean less tax revenue.

00:22:27.049 --> 00:22:30.210
Meanwhile, rising unemployment forces the central government to increase spending.

00:22:30.210 --> 00:22:33.960
Many unemployed people lack sufficient savings

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:36.419
and require government financial support.

00:22:36.419 --> 00:22:39.999
Furthermore, the government implements stimulus programs

00:22:39.999 --> 00:22:43.879
and increases spending to compensate for reduced economic activity.

00:22:43.879 --> 00:22:47.509
During deleveraging, the

00:22:47.509 --> 00:22:51.059
government's budget deficit soars because government spending exceeds tax revenue.

00:22:51.059 --> 00:22:55.720
This is the situation you hear about in the news regarding budget deficits.

00:22:55.720 --> 00:23:01.320
The government must raise taxes or borrow

00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:06.059
to fill the deficit. But, with declining incomes and many unemployed,

00:23:06.059 --> 00:23:09.789
where should it borrow from?

00:23:09.789 --> 00:23:14.580
Because the government needs more money, and a large amount of wealth is

00:23:14.580 --> 00:23:17.109
concentrated in the hands of a few,

00:23:17.109 --> 00:23:20.379
the government naturally increases taxes on the rich

00:23:20.379 --> 00:23:24.450
to help redistribute wealth in the economy,

00:23:24.450 --> 00:23:29.279
transferring wealth from the rich to the poor. The

00:23:29.279 --> 00:23:30.970
poor, already struggling, begin to resent the rich. The rich,

00:23:30.970 --> 00:23:36.309
burdened by a weak economy, asset devaluation, and increased taxes,

00:23:36.309 --> 00:23:39.940
begin to resent the poor.

00:23:39.940 --> 00:23:43.890
If the depression continues, social unrest will erupt. This will

00:23:43.890 --> 00:23:47.280
not only intensify tensions within countries

00:23:47.280 --> 00:23:52.200
but also between countries, especially between debtor and creditor nations.

00:23:52.200 --> 00:23:56.040
This situation can lead to political change,

00:23:56.040 --> 00:23:58.840
sometimes extreme change. In the

00:23:58.840 --> 00:24:02.630
1930s, this situation led to Hitler's rise to power, the

00:24:02.630 --> 00:24:08.080
outbreak of war in Europe, and the Great Depression in the United States,

00:24:08.080 --> 00:24:10.070
increasing the pressure to take action to end the depression.

00:24:10.070 --> 00:24:14.950
Don't forget that what people think of as money is actually mostly credit.

00:24:14.950 --> 00:24:18.400
Therefore, once credit disappears, people will not have enough money.

00:24:18.400 --> 00:24:23.190
People desperately need money, and you must remember, who can issue currency?

00:24:23.190 --> 00:24:27.370
Central banks can. Central

00:24:27.370 --> 00:24:30.309
banks have already lowered interest rates to near zero and

00:24:30.309 --> 00:24:34.090
now have to issue more currency. Issuing currency,

00:24:34.090 --> 00:24:37.299
unlike cutting spending, reducing debt, and redistributing wealth,

00:24:37.299 --> 00:24:42.190
will cause inflation and stimulate the economy. Central banks inevitably

00:24:42.190 --> 00:24:43.210
issue more money out of thin air

00:24:43.210 --> 00:24:47.150
and use that money to purchase financial assets

00:24:47.150 --> 00:24:52.260
and government bonds. This situation occurred during the Great Depression in the United States

00:24:52.260 --> 00:24:56.010
and resurfaced in 2008 when the Federal Reserve, the central bank of the United States,

00:24:56.010 --> 00:24:59.940
issued more than two trillion dollars in new currency.

00:24:59.940 --> 00:25:04.179
Other central banks around the world that could do the same also issued a lot of money.

00:25:04.179 --> 00:25:07.440
By using this money to buy financial assets, central banks

00:25:07.440 --> 00:25:11.679
helped push up asset prices, thereby increasing people's creditworthiness.

00:25:11.679 --> 00:25:15.820
However, this only benefits those who own financial assets.

00:25:15.820 --> 00:25:21.840
Central banks can issue currency, but they can only buy financial assets.

00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:24.900
On the other hand, central governments

00:25:24.900 --> 00:25:29.580
can buy goods and services and send money to the people,

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:34.520
but they cannot print money. Therefore, central banks and governments must cooperate in order to stimulate the economy

00:25:34.520 --> 00:25:35.650
. By

00:25:35.650 --> 00:25:40.470
purchasing government bonds, the central bank is essentially lending money to the government,

00:25:40.470 --> 00:25:41.190


00:25:41.190 --> 00:25:44.909
enabling it to run deficit budgets and increase spending on

00:25:44.909 --> 00:25:48.650
goods and services. It also

00:25:48.650 --> 00:25:53.080
increases people's income through stimulus programs and unemployment benefits,

00:25:53.080 --> 00:25:56.240
but this also increases government debt. However,

00:25:56.240 --> 00:25:59.540
this approach reduces the overall debt burden in the economy,

00:25:59.540 --> 00:26:05.429
which is a very risky time. Policymakers need to use the four methods of reducing debt burden in a balanced way. They

00:26:05.429 --> 00:26:06.590


00:26:06.590 --> 00:26:13.050
must balance and take into account both deflationary and inflationary methods

00:26:13.050 --> 00:26:14.710
in order to maintain stability.

00:26:14.710 --> 00:26:18.130
If a proper balance is achieved,

00:26:18.130 --> 00:26:21.479
harmonious deleveraging can be achieved.

00:26:21.479 --> 00:26:25.359
Therefore, deleveraging can be painful or harmonious.

00:26:25.359 --> 00:26:28.839
How can harmonious deleveraging be achieved?

00:26:28.839 --> 00:26:33.429
While deleveraging is difficult, it

00:26:33.429 --> 00:26:37.549
is a good thing to handle the difficult situation in the best possible way.

00:26:37.549 --> 00:26:42.210
This is much better than the excessive imbalances caused by excessive borrowing during the deleveraging phase.

00:26:42.210 --> 00:26:45.940
In a harmonious deleveraging process, the

00:26:45.940 --> 00:26:51.089
debt-to-income ratio decreases, and the economy actually grows positively. At the

00:26:51.089 --> 00:26:56.889
same time, inflation is not a problem; this is achieved through proper balance.

00:26:56.889 --> 00:27:00.159
To achieve proper balance, it is necessary to combine methods such as

00:27:00.159 --> 00:27:04.229
cutting spending, reducing debt and transferring wealth,

00:27:04.229 --> 00:27:09.149
and issuing currency to maintain economic and social stability.

00:27:09.149 --> 00:27:13.639
Some people ask whether issuing currency will exacerbate inflation?

00:27:13.639 --> 00:27:18.879
If the increase in money supply offsets the decrease in lending, it will not trigger inflation.
Don't forget, what matters is spending.

00:27:18.879 --> 00:27:24.249
Every dollar spent, whether in monetary or credit form, has the

00:27:24.249 --> 00:27:25.969
same impact on prices. The

00:27:25.969 --> 00:27:31.299
central bank can

00:27:31.299 --> 00:27:33.080
compensate for the disappearance of credit by increasing the money supply.

00:27:33.080 --> 00:27:38.509
To reverse the situation, the central bank not only needs to

00:27:38.509 --> 00:27:39.519
promote income growth

00:27:39.519 --> 00:27:43.849
but also needs to ensure that the income growth rate exceeds the

00:27:43.849 --> 00:27:45.099
interest rate on accumulated debt.

00:27:45.099 --> 00:27:48.379
What does this mean? The main point is that

00:27:48.379 --> 00:27:52.309
income must grow faster than debt. For example,

00:27:52.309 --> 00:27:56.080
let's assume a country is deleveraging,

00:27:56.080 --> 00:27:58.580
with an income ratio of 100%.

00:27:58.580 --> 00:28:03.659
This means the debt is equivalent to the

00:28:03.659 --> 00:28:05.799
country's annual income. Let's

00:28:05.799 --> 00:28:09.009
assume the interest rate on this debt

00:28:09.009 --> 00:28:11.159
is 2%.

00:28:11.159 --> 00:28:15.029
If debt increases at a rate of 2%

00:28:15.029 --> 00:28:15.690
while income

00:28:15.690 --> 00:28:20.230
growth is only about 1%, the debt burden will never ease.

00:28:20.230 --> 00:28:24.809
More money must be issued to make income growth

00:28:24.809 --> 00:28:25.950
exceed the interest rate.

00:28:25.950 --> 00:28:30.720
However, issuing money is too easy, this method can be easily abused,

00:28:30.720 --> 00:28:33.049
and it is more popular than other methods.

00:28:33.049 --> 00:28:36.200
Therefore, the key is to avoid issuing too much money, which can

00:28:36.200 --> 00:28:41.110
lead to hyperinflation, as seen in Germany's

00:28:41.110 --> 00:28:43.129
deleveraging in the 1920s.

00:28:43.129 --> 00:28:48.269
If policymakers strike the right balance, the deleveraging process will not be so drastic.

00:28:48.269 --> 00:28:51.340
Economic growth will be slow, but the debt burden will decrease.

00:28:51.340 --> 00:28:54.470
This is harmonious deleveraging.

00:28:54.470 --> 00:28:59.830
When income rises, borrowers' creditworthiness improves.

00:28:59.830 --> 00:29:02.999
Once borrowers appear more creditworthy,

00:29:02.999 --> 00:29:08.509
lenders will begin to resume lending, and the debt burden will finally begin to decrease.

00:29:08.509 --> 00:29:13.499
People can increase their consumption when they can borrow money. The economy has finally

00:29:13.499 --> 00:29:14.399
begun to recover, and the

00:29:14.399 --> 00:29:18.009
long-term debt cycle has entered a reflation phase. Deleveraging,

00:29:18.009 --> 00:29:22.490
if handled improperly, can be disastrous,

00:29:22.490 --> 00:29:26.279
but if handled well, it will ultimately solve the problem. It will

00:29:26.279 --> 00:29:29.580
take approximately ten years or more

00:29:29.580 --> 00:29:33.669
for the debt burden to decrease and economic activity to return to normal;

00:29:33.669 --> 00:29:37.899
hence the term "the lost decade." In

00:29:37.899 --> 00:29:42.720
summary, the economy is certainly more complex than this model suggests.

00:29:42.720 --> 00:29:43.639


00:29:43.639 --> 00:29:48.850
However, by combining the short-term debt cycle with the long-term debt cycle

00:29:48.850 --> 00:29:52.629
and then with the productivity growth trajectory,

00:29:52.629 --> 00:29:55.919
we arrive at a good model that clarifies our past and present

00:29:55.919 --> 00:29:58.919
situation and potential future directions.

00:29:58.919 --> 00:30:03.019
Finally, I hope everyone learns three rules of thumb:

00:30:03.019 --> 00:30:03.720


00:30:03.720 --> 00:30:08.220
First, don't let debt grow faster than income,

00:30:08.220 --> 00:30:11.429
because the debt burden will eventually crush you.

00:30:11.429 --> 00:30:16.059
Second, don't let income grow faster than productivity,

00:30:16.059 --> 00:30:19.159
because this will ultimately make you uncompetitive.

00:30:19.159 --> 00:30:24.000
Third, do everything possible to improve productivity,

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:29.029
because productivity plays the most crucial role in the long run.

00:30:29.549 --> 00:30:34.080
This is my simple advice to everyone, and to policymakers.

00:30:34.080 --> 00:30:38.470
You may be surprised to find that most people, including most policymakers, haven't given this enough attention.

00:30:38.470 --> 00:30:39.509


00:30:39.509 --> 00:30:44.059
This model has been very useful to me, and I hope it will be useful to you as well.

00:30:44.059 --> 00:30:46.380
Thank you.

